{%- comment -%}
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Which collection a product's breadcrumb should name.

  Echoes a collection handle, or nothing. Callers do:

      {%- capture bc_handle -%}
        {%- render 'breadcrumb-collection', product: product -%}
      {%- endcapture -%}
      {%- assign bc_collection = collections[bc_handle | strip] -%}

  WHY THIS EXISTS
  `product.collections | first` returns an arbitrary collection, and most of
  this catalogue's products sit in merchandising groups as well as garment ones
  — "Best Sellers", "Sale 2026", "All Products - Discount Scope". A breadcrumb
  reading Home › All Products - Discount Scope › Moksha is worse than no
  breadcrumb: it tells the shopper nothing about where they are and hands Google
  a nonsense trail. So the garment collections are named explicitly, in order,
  and everything else is ignored.

  The order only matters if a product ever lands in two garment collections. At
  the time of writing none does — the 92 products divide cleanly, which is what
  makes this resolution unambiguous rather than a guess. If that changes, the
  first match here wins, so keep the most specific categories at the front.

  Sleeve-length collections come last deliberately: they cut across the garment
  types (a Full Sleeve piece is usually also a Sweatshirt), so they are only the
  right answer for something that belongs to no garment collection at all.

  A product matching nothing here echoes an empty string, and the breadcrumb
  drops its middle step rather than inventing one. Three products are in that
  position today — duskline, memento-vitae and ombrix — and the honest fix is
  to put them in a collection, not to make this snippet guess.
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- liquid
  assign bc_priority = 'polo,shirts,hoodie,sweatshirt,t-shirts,sleeve-less,full-sleeve,half-sleeve' | split: ','
  assign bc_match = ''
  for bc_handle in bc_priority
    if bc_match == ''
      for bc_candidate in product.collections
        if bc_candidate.handle == bc_handle
          assign bc_match = bc_handle
          break
        endif
      endfor
    endif
  endfor
  echo bc_match
-%}
